(() => {
 const qa={
 overview:[['Can a zero-value swap need initial margin?','Yes. Zero current value says nothing about the range of adverse values during close-out.'],['Does VM eliminate the need for IM?','VM covers the current exposure snapshot; market risk can develop after that snapshot.'],['Is IM a guarantee against every loss?','No. It is a risk buffer calibrated to a coverage objective, not an unlimited guarantee.']],
 horizon:[['Why is a daily price move not the whole close-out risk?','Replacing, hedging and closing positions takes time, during which markets can keep moving.'],['Does 99% coverage mean one breach in every hundred observations exactly?','No. It is a model target; finite samples fluctuate and dependent observations complicate interpretation.'],['When can the square-root rule mislead?','Jumps, persistent volatility, illiquidity and dependence can invalidate independent Gaussian scaling.']],
 hierarchy:[['Can the rate risk of an equity option automatically offset the rate risk of a swap?','Not across different SIMM product classes. The equity option’s rate risk remains in the Equity product class.'],['Are there four risk classes?','There are four product classes and six risk classes. They play different roles in aggregation.'],['Can I choose the cheapest product classification?','No. Classification follows the instrument and methodology, not the desired margin result.']],
 units:[['Why should counterparties agree on the bump before comparing risk?','The same reported number can mean per bp, per percent, or per larger shock, producing very different weighted exposures.'],['Should a negative sensitivity be made positive immediately?','No. Its sign carries hedge information through eligible netting and covariance aggregation.'],['Does currency conversion belong only at the final total?','Sensitivity amounts and concentration thresholds must be comparable in consistent currencies before threshold testing.']],
 netting:[['Why does gross processing overstate an exact hedge?','It charges both positions without recognising that their signed risk to the same eligible factor cancels.'],['Is a similar maturity enough for exact netting?','No. Different tenors or curve qualifiers are different factors, with offsets recognised through the relevant correlations.'],['Can trades with different counterparties cancel in one margin set?','Not merely because their market exposures offset. Legal netting boundaries must be respected.']],
 concentration:[['Is the threshold a limit on trade notional?','No. It applies to the prescribed sensitivity group in the required currency and units.'],['Why does a large position grow faster than linearly?','Above threshold, both the sensitivity and its square-root concentration factor increase.'],['Is the same concentration group used everywhere?','No. IR, credit and other classes have different grouping rules, and vega has separate thresholds.']],
 bucket:[['Why does higher correlation help an opposite-sign hedge?','More common movement makes gains on one side more likely to offset losses on the other.'],['Why can higher correlation hurt same-sign positions?','They are more likely to lose together, so the aggregation recognises less diversification.'],['Does zero net sensitivity imply zero bucket risk?','Only for exact eligible factor offsets or appropriate perfect correlation; different factors can move apart.']],
 'correlation-adjustment':[['Why is concentration not just a bigger risk weight?','For non-IR factors it also changes the correlation adjustment when concentration factors differ.'],['When is the adjustment equal to one?','When the two concentration factors are equal.'],['Does this exact adjustment sit inside every IR currency bucket?','No. IR shares concentration within currency and uses the corresponding adjustment across currencies.']],
 'across-buckets':[['Why carry both K and S?','K describes magnitude; S provides bounded direction for cross-bucket interaction.'],['Why not use an unlimited signed sum?','Its magnitude could exceed the bucket risk and distort the next aggregation layer.'],['Can a residual bucket hedge ordinary buckets through the normal cross term?','Where the residual rule applies, its margin is added separately outside the ordinary-bucket square root.']],
 ir:[['Why can a swap hedge leave risk in the same currency?','Its maturity, curve or inflation exposure can differ from the position being hedged.'],['What joins the concentration sum?','Eligible ordinary rates and inflation sensitivities in the currency; cross-currency basis is excluded.'],['Is every currency assigned the same risk weight?','No. Currency volatility category and tenor matter in the prescribed calibration.']],
 'credit-q':[['Can two bonds from one issuer always net completely?','Seniority, tenor and other risk identifiers must be matched; different curves are not identical risk.'],['Is high-yield corporate risk automatically non-qualifying?','No. Credit quality affects bucketing; non-qualifying is not a catch-all label for weak credit.'],['What risk can a tranche add beyond spreads?','Sensitivity to base correlation can create an additional Credit Qualifying margin component.']],
 'credit-nq':[['Why keep qualifying and non-qualifying credit separate?','They have different risk definitions and calibration, and combine only at the appropriate risk-class layer.'],['Does securitisation risk depend only on average borrower quality?','Structure, tranche exposure and credit-spread mapping matter as well.'],['Can this applet value a tranche?','No. It begins with supplied sensitivities and teaches a reduced aggregation mechanism.']],
 equity:[['Why can an index hedge leave single-name risk?','The stock can move differently from the index, and its concentration may remain.'],['Is a volatility index treated exactly like an ordinary stock?','No. The methodology has special risk-weight and curvature rules for the volatility-index bucket.'],['What can change an equity bucket?','Characteristics such as market, size and sector affect mapping under the methodology.']],
 commodity:[['Why might two oil positions hedge imperfectly?','Grade, location and delivery conditions can expose them to different prices.'],['Is commodity risk one global factor?','No. Commodity definitions, buckets and correlations distinguish multiple drivers.'],['Can the same ordinary-bucket formula be assumed for every exception?','No. Other and index treatments and the prescribed calibration must be checked.']],
 fx:[['Why does FX delta depend on calculation currency?','Currency sensitivities are expressed against that reference currency.'],['Is FX vega represented in the same way as FX delta?','No. Vega is associated with currency pairs, while delta is currency exposure against the calculation currency.'],['Can I just relabel USD amounts as EUR?','No. Amounts, sensitivities and thresholds require consistent transformation and conversion.']],
 vega:[['Why multiply vega by volatility?','A quoted sensitivity alone is not the standardised volatility-risk exposure used by SIMM.'],['Is HVR used identically for rates and equities?','No. Non-IR HVR enters the specified vega exposure transformation; IR has its own curvature scaling treatment.'],['Can delta concentration thresholds be reused for vega?','No. Vega has its own thresholds and prescribed concentration groups.']],
 curvature:[['Why can equal vegas with different expiries leave curvature?','Each expiry is scaled before netting, so the resulting CVRs need not cancel.'],['Should I feed gamma directly into this formula?','No. SIMM builds the specified curvature proxy from volatility-weighted vega and expiry.'],['Why does reversing both signs matter?','Theta and the signed CVR sum change, producing an asymmetric final curvature charge.']],
 basis:[['Why exclude cross-currency basis from ordinary concentration?','The methodology gives it specific treatment; adding it to the ordinary sum would apply the wrong concentration rule.'],['Does inflation disappear into the nominal yield curve?','No. It has a separate risk factor and weight even though it joins the relevant IR concentration grouping.'],['Does the chart use the full IR covariance matrix?','No. Its simplified correlations isolate the concentration exclusion mechanism.']],
 'base-correlation':[['Is base correlation the same as the SIMM aggregation correlation?','No. One is a risk factor affecting tranche value; the other is a prescribed parameter combining sensitivities.'],['Why can offsetting families leave risk?','Different index families are not treated as the same factor and have less than perfect correlation.'],['Should BC01 receive a concentration uplift?','No. The specified base-correlation treatment excludes it from concentration risk.']],
 indices:[['Why does constituent detail help explain a hedge?','It reveals which part of index risk matches the single-name position and which part remains.'],['Can I apply delta decomposition assumptions to every index vega?','No. Vega and curvature have specific index treatments to verify separately.'],['What should happen to an unknown mapping?','Investigate the data and apply the correct classification rules, including residual treatment where required.']],
 total:[['Can delta offset curvature by subtracting their final charges?','No. Non-negative component margins are added within the risk class.'],['Where does risk-class diversification occur?','Within each product class using the prescribed cross-risk-class matrix.'],['Can Credit and Equity product-class totals diversify at the final step?','No. Product-class totals are summed.']],
 incremental:[['Can a risky trade reduce margin?','Yes. It can offset an existing portfolio exposure even though its standalone margin is positive.'],['Is the biggest hedge always the cheapest outcome?','No. Over-hedging can create a new exposure and concentration.'],['Is a negative increment the same as a negative margin requirement?','No. It means the new requirement is smaller than the original requirement.']],
 addons:[['Why keep an add-on separate from sensitivities?','It preserves a clear explanation of model risk or remediation rather than disguising an adjustment as market risk.'],['Does the multiplier contribution equal scale times SIMM?','The additional contribution is only scale minus one times SIMM; the base is already counted.'],['Can opposite notionals automatically net in the notional add-on?','The specified affected-product calculation uses gross absolute current notionals.']],
 collateral:[['Can a threshold reduce the model’s estimate of risk?','No. It changes the operational collateral obligation under the applicable rules and agreement.'],['Why can a haircut increase the securities needed?','Only the haircut-adjusted value is recognised against the requirement.'],['Can a group threshold be copied onto every agreement?','No. Allocation must respect consolidated-group limits and applicable documentation.']],
 'two-way':[['Does receiving as much IM as you post mean no funding need?','Not when received IM is unavailable for reuse. Posting still consumes your funding.'],['Why can the two directions have different requirements?','Sign-sensitive components such as curvature and directional portfolio details can make amounts differ.'],['Is the simple funding proxy a complete MVA?','No. MVA requires future IM and funding assumptions over time, with valuation conventions and discounting.']],
 crif:[['Does using CRIF guarantee correct margin?','No. A consistent format can still carry missing trades, wrong units or incorrect mapping.'],['Why reconcile qualifiers before totals?','An unmatched factor identity prevents correct netting and explains downstream differences.'],['Should a disagreement be fixed by replacing the final number?','No. Identify the population, risk, currency or model-version source of the discrepancy.']],
 regridding:[['Why avoid nearest-tenor snapping?','It can create artificial jumps and misrepresent exposure around bucket boundaries.'],['Does preserving the sum prove the transformation is correct?','No. The transformed risk must also preserve the intended price behaviour under relevant curve movements.'],['Why can weighted totals change with tenor?','Different vertices can have different weights and correlations even when unweighted risk is conserved.']],
 backtesting:[['Can one severe breach matter despite high observed coverage?','Yes. Severity exposes shortfall magnitude that breach frequency alone hides.'],['Why are overlapping ten-day observations tricky?','They share market days and therefore are not independent evidence.'],['Does this chart certify a governance pass?','No. It is synthetic and omits the full monitoring, assessment and remediation framework.']],
 versions:[['Why rerun frozen risk through both versions?','It isolates calibration effects from new trades and market-driven sensitivity changes.'],['Can the same trade population produce a different margin after a release?','Yes. Weights, thresholds and correlations can change.'],['What evidence should accompany a version change?','Record both versions, effective dates, frozen inputs, parameter changes, attribution and validation results.']]
 };
 window.LEARNING_CONTENT=Object.fromEntries(SIMM_LESSONS.map(l=>[l.id,{intuition:[l.simple,'Predict the direction of the change before moving a control. Then connect the chart to the signed inputs and intermediate calculation rows.'],questions:[...qa[l.id],['What should I predict before trying the suggested experiment?',l.challenge+' Write down your expectation, change only one input, and explain any difference using the trace.'],['What does the formula reveal that the headline number hides?',l.formula+' The intermediate rows expose the ordering, signs and adjustments behind the total.'],['What is deliberately simplified in this experiment?',l.scope],['Which quantities here are assumptions rather than universal constants?',l.key+' Check the control labels and scope for any explicitly prescribed calibration values.'],['How could I test whether a surprising result is a data issue?','Reset, check signs and units, verify factor identity and product/netting boundaries, then change one input at a time.'],['How would I explain this mechanism to a colleague without equations?',l.simple],['What must I verify before applying this example to a real portfolio?','Consult the linked source, confirm the effective version and full risk mapping, and validate the omitted exceptions. Save inputs and assumptions alongside the calculation.']]}]));
})();
